@node M4 emulation example
@chapter M4 emulation example

@section Introduction

The @code{m4} emulation is an example of defining a macro language in @code{m0}.
It shows the different possibilities and solutions possible with @code{m0}.

It is (at least now) not a goal to be 100% compatible with @code{m4} or to be complete.

Since @code{m0} will also detect macros with invalid characters of @code{m4} macros, it will
probably be very difficult to be 100% compatible for cases where certain characteristics of
@code{m4} are used.

Also functions for regular expressions in @code{m0} seem not necessary as this functionality can also
be accomplished with patterns.

@section Emulation

The following text of the emulation is generated from a source file for both text and code.
From this source file different versions of @code{m4} are generated. These versions are:

@table @code
@item v7
The original version of @code{m4}, see @cite{The M4 Macro Processor, Brian W. Kernighan and Dennis M. Ritchie, Bell Laboratories, July 1, 1977}.
@item posix
A version with only the macros defined by posix, see e.g. @url{https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/utilities/m4.html, m4}.
@item gnu
Version with the macros defined in GNU @code{m4}, see @url{https://www.gnu.org/software/m4/manual/m4.html,GNU M4 manual}.
@item bsd
Version with the macros defined in @code{m4} of BSD, see e.g. @url{https://man.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?query=m4&sektion=1&format=html, FreeBSD m4 manual page}.
@end table

Macros or code for all versions is indicated with @code{all}.

@subsection Main design

The emulation of @code{m4} is using three macro sets:
@enumerate
@item The default macro set @code{0} is used to define all basic macros that are necessary to
be able to define the @code{m4} macros. It is also used to hold some @code{m4} macros that are
build using the basic macros. The emulation starts in this macro set and is switched to the @code{m4exec} macro set 
to start emulation of the @code{m4} input.
@item The @code{m4} macros are defined and executed in the macro set @code{m4exec}.
@item The @code{m4builtin} macro set is used as a collection of builtin @code{m4} macros that
can be called from the @code{builtin} macro.
@end enumerate

A big number of the builtin @code{m4} macros are directly linked to the functions in @code{m0}.
Several patterns are used to implement the syntax of @code{m4}.

Some builtin @code{m4} macros are emulated using a collection of macros in the default macro set @code{0}.

Quoting and comments are implemented as macros as there exists no built in functionality for this in @code{m0}.

Although it it not necessary to use three macro sets, it allows a relatively easy separation of the underlying macro code and the emulated
macros. Another solution can be a proper naming of underlying macros such that these are unlikely accidentally called.

